STRIDE & BASE | Issue 212 | December 19, 2025

THIS WEEK: The case for embarrassing pace

If your easy runs are the subject of mild ridicule from the people you pass, =
you might be doing them right. Most recreational runners compress their train=
ing into a narrow band between "comfortable" and "comfortably hard", and the =
cost is cumulative: too tired to run the hard days hard, too fit to get much =
out of the easy ones.

The physiology isn't mysterious. Mitochondrial density, capillary networks an=
d fat oxidation all respond to volume at low intensity, and they respond slow=
ly, over months and years rather than a training block. The runners who sit a=
t 70-75% of max heart rate for 80% of their week aren't sandbagging. They are=
 paying into an account that compounds.

Three practical checks for the holiday weeks:
- The talk test. If you can't say a full sentence without a breath in the mid=
dle, slow down.
- Heart-rate drift. Run 60 minutes at a steady pace and compare the first and=
 last 20 minutes. Drift above 5% means the pace wasn't really easy yet.
- Hills. Walk them if you have to. Nobody is grading you.

ALSO IN THIS ISSUE
- Reader question: "I was a miler in college and I can't run slowly without f=
eeling like a fraud." Short answer: the fraud is the 6:40 pace you hold every=
 day.
- Gear: we wore twelve winter gloves in 20F wind. The two winners are both un=
der $40.
- Race calendar: spring halves that open registration in January.

Run easy, run often.
The Stride & Base editors

